From bcfdfaf7f46fc589cff25e5a7d746ee27ae18816 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 20:59:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/11] Files on AROS, over dos.library

Open, Read, Write, Close, Rename, DeleteFile, CreateDir and Lock, with
a layer over them that does not treat dos.library like POSIX.

Read and Write may do less than asked, and a short count is neither an
error nor the end of the file, so both are looped on. Closing a file
being written is where a handler can still say the data did not land,
so it is checked rather than left to a drop.

Rename refuses a target that already exists. Measured on AROS One's
AROS: volume (SFS-BE) rather than assumed: 'Can't rename :t/a as :t/b
because object already exists', and the failed rename changed nothing.
So there is no atomic replace to build on, and fs::replace does the
dance by hand - write beside, close, move the old aside, move the new
in, drop the old. What that buys is that a failure at any step leaves a
complete copy of the previous save; what it cannot promise is that the
real name always exists, and the doc comment says so.
